 decorations and statuary. Its \"English square\", or Cimetière Anglais, is the final resting place for a number of English people who made Cannes their home.","wpu":"Cimetière du Grand Jas","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Grand-Jas_Derviz.jpg/960px-Grand-Jas_Derviz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hôtel Martinez","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.5475,"lo":7.030556,"d":1441,"ds":"The Hôtel Martinez is an Art Deco hotel on the Promenade de la Croisette in Cannes. It was opened on 20 February 1929 by Emmanuel Michele Martinez, son of Baron Giovanni Martinez and Giuseppa Labiso Costanza, members of a noble Italian family of Spanish origins from Palermo. Karan Singh, the crown prince of Jammu and Kashmir, was born there in 1931.","wpu":"Hôtel Martinez","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4a/H%C3%B4tel_Martinez%2C_Cannes_%282014%29.JPG/960px-H%C3%B4tel_Martinez%2C_Cannes_%282014%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Château Thorenc","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.564,"lo":7.0289,"d":2016,"ds":"The Château Thorenc is a historic garden in Cannes, France. It was established in 1870 for the Duchess of Bedford, when a chateau was erected. It was acquired by Sir Richard Atwood Glass in 1876.","wpu":"Château Thorenc","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Cannes_-_Ch%C3%A2teau_Thorenc_01.JPG/960px-Cannes_-_Ch%C3%A2teau_Thorenc_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Michael the Archangel Church (Cannes)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.546111,"lo":7.039722,"d":2196,"ds":"St. Michael the Archangel Church, Cannes is an Eastern Orthodox church in Cannes, France (40 Boulevard Alexandre III). It was built in 1894.","wpu":"St. Michael the Archangel Church (Cannes)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/ACOR-Cannes.jpg/960px-ACOR-Cannes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hôtel de Ville, Le Cannet","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.5766,"lo":7.0191,"d":2998,"ds":"The Hôtel de Ville (French pronunciation: [otɛl də vil], City Hall) is a municipal building in Le Cannet, Alpes-Maritimes, in southeastern France, standing on Boulevard Sadi Carnot.","wpu":"Hôtel de Ville, Le Cannet","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/La_mairie_du_Cannet.JPG/960px-La_mairie_du_Cannet.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Île Sainte-Marguerite","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.519444,"lo":7.05,"d":4521,"ds":"Île Sainte-Marguerite (pronounced [il sɛ̃tmaʁɡ(ə)ʁit]; French: Île Sainte-Marguerite, transl. Saint Margaret Island) is the largest of the Lérins Islands, about half a mile offshore from the French Riviera city of Cannes, situated in the Bay of Cannes. The island is approximately 3,200 metres (2.0 miles) in length (east to west) and 950 metres (0.59 miles) across (north to south).","wpu":"Île Sainte-Marguerite","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Plan_%C3%AEle_Sainte-Marguerite.JPG/960px-Plan_%C3%AEle_Sainte-Marguerite.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Île Saint-Honorat","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.507778,"lo":7.045833,"d":5389,"ds":"Île Saint-Honorat (French pronunciation: [il sɛ̃tɔnɔʁa]) is the second-largest of the Lérins Islands, about 1.6 kilometres (1 mi) off shore from the French Riviera city of Cannes. The island is approximately 1.5 kilometres (0.93 mi) in length (east to west) and 400 metres (1,300 ft) wide. Since the 5th century, the island has been home to a community of monks.","wpu":"Île Saint-Honorat","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Aerial_tour_of_the_Cote_d%27Azur_-_panoramio_-_Alistair_Cunningham_%2825%29.jpg/960px-Aerial_tour_of_the_Cote_d%27Azur_-_panoramio_-_Alistair_Cunningham_%2825%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lérins Abbey","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.506389,"lo":7.0475,"d":5590,"ds":"Lérins Abbey is a Cistercian monastery on the island of Saint-Honorat, one of the Lérins Islands, on the French Riviera, with an active monastic community. There has been a monastic community there since the 5th century. The construction of the current monastery buildings began around 1073.","wpu":"Lérins Abbey","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/%D0%9B%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B2_%D0%AD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C.jpg/960px-%D0%9B%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B2_%D0%AD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mougins Museum of Classical Art","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.601316,"lo":6.99485,"d":5890,"ds":"The MACM, the Mougins Museum of Classical Art (Musée d’Art Classique de Mougins) was a private museum inaugurated in June 2011 in the village of Mougins in the Alpes-Maritimes department, France. In August 2023, the MACAM closed to make way for a new museum, the Female Artists of Mougins Museum (FAMM), which opened in June 2024. The museum's director was Leisa Paoli.","wpu":"Mougins Museum of Classical Art","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/MACM.JPG/960px-MACM.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Château de la Napoule","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.523333,"lo":6.943056,"d":6370,"ds":"The Château de la Napoule is a restored French castle, located in Mandelieu-la-Napoule in the Alpes-Maritimes Department of France, It has been classified as an historical landmark since 1993, and the gardens are listed by the French Ministry of Culture as among the notable gardens of France. It was featured as one of the main locations in the 1999 movie Simon Sez.","wpu":"Château de la Napoule","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/La_Napoule_Ch%C3%A2teau_vue_de_la_Raguette.jpg/960px-La_Napoule_Ch%C3%A2teau_vue_de_la_Raguette.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hôtel de Ville, Mandelieu-la-Napoule","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.5427,"lo":6.9318,"d":6594,"ds":"The Hôtel de Ville (French pronunciation: [otɛl də vil], City Hall) is a municipal building in Mandelieu-la-Napoule, Alpes-Maritimes, in southeastern France, standing on Rue Jean Monnet.","wpu":"Hôtel de Ville, Mandelieu-la-Napoule","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Waldorf_Hotel%2C_Mandelieu-la-Napoule.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Théoule-sur-Mer Castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.508142,"lo":6.939697,"d":7522,"ds":"Théoule-sur-Mer Castle (French: Château de Théoule-sur-Mer) is a castle located in Théoule-sur-Mer, France.","wpu":"Théoule-sur-Mer Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/575_Th%C3%A9oule.jpg/960px-575_Th%C3%A9oule.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sophia Antipolis","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.622222,"lo":7.05,"d":8565,"ds":"Sophia Antipolis is a 2,400 hectare technology park in southeast France, and as of 2021 home to 2,500 companies, valued today at more than 5.6 billion euros and employing more than 38,000 people counting more than 80 nationalities. The park is known to be Europe's first science and technology hub. The technology park is also a platform, cluster and creation-hub for start-ups.","wpu":"Sophia Antipolis","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Sophia_Antipolis.jpg/960px-Sophia_Antipolis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Villa Aujourd'hui","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.551,"lo":7.1198,"d":8607,"ds":"The villa Aujourd'hui is a Modernist villa built by the American architect Barry Dierks (1899–1960) for the Palm Beach, Florida, socialite, Mrs. Audrey Chadwick.","wpu":"Villa Aujourd'hui","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Port_de_l%27Olivette%2C_Villa_Aujourd%27hui.jpg/960px-Port_de_l%27Olivette%2C_Villa_Aujourd%27hui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palais Bulles","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.4886,"lo":6.94363,"d":8826,"ds":"The Palais Bulles (French pronunciation: [palɛ byl]; lit. 'Bubbles Palace') is an iconoclastic villa in Théoule-sur-Mer, near Cannes, on the French Riviera, which was designed by the Hungarian architect Antti Lovag. It was built for the French industrialist Pierre Bernard, and later bought by the fashion designer Pierre Cardin as a holiday home.","wpu":"Palais Bulles","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/Th%C3%A9oule-sur-Mer-Miramar%2C_Le_Paleis_Bulles_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Jardin botanique de la Villa Thuret","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.563889,"lo":7.124444,"d":9112,"ds":"The Jardin botanique de la Villa Thuret (3.5 hectares (8.6 acres)) is a renowned botanical garden located on the grounds of the Villa Thuret, 90, chemin Raymond, Antibes Juan-les-Pins, Alpes-Maritimes, Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, France. It is open weekdays without charge.","wpu":"Jardin botanique de la Villa Thuret","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/General_view_-_Villa_Thuret_-_DSC04779.JPG/960px-General_view_-_Villa_Thuret_-_DSC04779.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Cannes is a resort city located on the French Riviera. It is located in the Alpes-Maritimes department of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, and is the host city of the annual Cannes Film Festival, Midem, and Cannes Lions International Festival of Creativity. The city is known for its association with the rich and famous, its luxury hotels and restaurants, and for several conferences.","wpu":"Cannes","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Cannes_from_Suquet_Tower_03.jpg/960px-Cannes_from_Suquet_Tower_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}